    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here are a few key drivers of the wealth inequality in the US over the past 40 years.\n\nFirst, the **decline of union power and the shift in the labor market** have been critical, leading to wage stagnation for the majority of the American workforce while the gains in productivity have gone to capital owners. Second, **tax and regulatory policy changes** have often favored high-net-worth individuals, such as preferential capital gains tax rates and the reduction of top marginal tax rates, which allows for greater wealth accumulation without the same strain on the overall tax base.\n\nFinally, **globalization and the rise of the \"superstar firm\" economy** have allowed a very small segment of the population in highly specialized, high-demand fields to command extreme, often winner-take-all, levels of income.",
